        Robust Context-Aware Object Recognition
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Janouskova%2C+K">Klara Janouskova</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Gavrus%2C+C">Cristian Gavrus</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Matas%2C+J">Jiri Matas</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2510.00618v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;play an important role. However, standard supervised learning often leads to unintended over-reliance on the BG, known as shortcut learning of spurious correlations, limiting <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span> robustness in real-&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2510.00618v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2510.00618v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2510.00618v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        In visual recognition, both the object of interest (referred to as foreground, FG, for simplicity) and its surrounding context (background, BG) play an important role. However, standard supervised learning often leads to unintended over-reliance on the BG, known as shortcut learning of spurious correlations, limiting <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span> robustness in real-<span class="search-hit mathjax">world</span> deployment settings. In the literature, the problem is mainly addressed by suppressing the BG, sacrificing context information for improved generalization.
  We propose RCOR -- Robust Context-Aware Object Recognition -- the first approach that jointly achieves robustness and context-awareness without compromising either. RCOR treats localization as an integral part of recognition to decouple object-centric and context-aware <span class="search-hit mathjax">modelling</span>, followed by a robust, non-parametric fusion. It improves the performance of both supervised <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> and VLM on datasets with both in-domain and out-of-domain BG, even without fine-tuning. The results confirm that localization before recognition is now possible even in complex scenes as in ImageNet-1k.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2510.00618v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2510.00618v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 1 October, 2025; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2025.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
